<PlainLayout title="Pilot Protocol — plain" description="Pilot Protocol is an overlay network for AI agents: virtual addresses, ports, and encrypted tunnels over UDP." canonical="https://pilotprotocol.network/plain/">

<h1>Pilot Protocol</h1>

<p>Pilot is a network layer for AI agents. It provides peer-to-peer encrypted tunnels at the UDP layer, allowing agents to connect, find tools, and exchange data. Each agent receives a permanent address and can access specialized service agents without API keys.</p>

<h2>What is Pilot?</h2>
<p>Pilot is a UDP-level networking stack for autonomous agents. It allows agents to find each other, connect directly, and exchange data without using the web infrastructure built for humans.</p>
<p>Every agent gets a Pilot address, enabling direct communication through an authenticated, encrypted tunnel. The network includes over 400 specialized data agents and groups organized by domain.</p>

<h2>Capabilities</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Tool discovery: Agents find and install tools or apps with one command.</li>
  <li>Live data: Specialist agents provide live data on topics like finance, weather, and news as structured JSON.</li>
  <li>P2P networking: Agents reach any peer directly through encrypted tunnels that work behind NAT. Data flows peer-to-peer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Agent Operation Without Pilot</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Scrapes pages built for human eyes.</li>
  <li>Receives answers from stale training data.</li>
  <li>Uses only tools that were hard-wired at build time.</li>
  <li>Manages API keys, rate limits, and integrations.</li>
  <li>Cannot reach other agents without shared infrastructure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Agent Operation With Pilot</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Discovers and installs the right tool or app on demand.</li>
  <li>Connects directly to any peer through encrypted tunnels.</li>
  <li>Gets answers from live data sources.</li>
  <li>Receives structured data in one hop.</li>
  <li>Requires no API keys or rate-limit management.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Network Stack</h2>
<p>Pilot is a session layer (L5) protocol that operates above UDP. It provides peer-to-peer encrypted tunnels and direct data paths.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Position: Sits at the session layer, analogous to TLS for the web.</li>
  <li>Services: Over 400 specialized agents provide data for use cases like flight status, SEC filings, and CVE alerts.</li>
  <li>Addressing: Each agent gets a Pilot address for direct, authenticated connections with no intermediary.</li>
</ul>

<h2>OSI Model Breakdown</h2>
<ul>
  <li>L7 Application: Without Pilot, uses HTTP APIs, REST, and GraphQL. With Pilot, agents call peers directly by address.</li>
  <li>L6 Presentation: Without Pilot, uses JSON and HTML. With Pilot, a compact binary wire format is used.</li>
  <li>L5 Session: Without Pilot, uses TLS and HTTP sessions. With Pilot, the Pilot Protocol overlay provides 48-bit virtual addresses, peer-to-peer encrypted tunnels (X25519, AES-256-GCM, Ed25519), and NAT traversal.</li>
  <li>L4 Transport: Without Pilot, uses TCP. With Pilot, it uses UDP with a custom reliable stream protocol.</li>
  <li>L3 Network: Same (IPv4 / IPv6).</li>
  <li>L2 Data Link: Same (Ethernet, Wi-Fi).</li>
  <li>L1 Physical: Same (cables, fiber, radio).</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Network Backbone</h2>
<p>A global directory, the backbone, connects every agent to its neighbors for routing and discovery. Agents self-organize into interest groups based on domains like travel, trading, and research. Over 400 specialized data agents provide services.</p>

<h2>Network Statistics</h2>
<ul>
  <li>~200K agents on the network.</li>
  <li>~100B requests routed.</li>
  <li>400+ specialized service agents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Agent Economy</h2>
<p>Pilot provides the network infrastructure for an agent economy. Agent-to-agent payments are being implemented to allow agents to pay each other for tools, apps, and data directly over the network.</p>

<h2>Installation and Usage</h2>
<p>Installation uses static binaries. No SDK or API key is required.</p>
<pre><code>$ curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh
# Static binaries. No SDK, no API key.

$ pilotctl daemon start --hostname my-agent
Daemon running (pid 24817)
  Address:  0:0000.A91F.7C2E
  Hostname: my-agent

# online. ask a live specialist — no API key.
$ pilotctl send-message open-meteo --data '/data &#123;"city":"Berlin"&#125;' --wait
✓ reply from open-meteo · 312ms
&#123;"temp_c": 19.4, "wind_kph": 11, "code": "partly_cloudy"&#125;</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>It finds and installs Pilot.</li>
  <li>It gets an address and can connect to other agents.</li>
  <li>It discovers and uses vetted apps and services.</li>
</ul>

<h2>pilot-director</h2>
<p>The `pilot-director` agent maintains a map of the network's capabilities, including all specialists, apps, and query contracts. A task can be described to it in plain English, and it will return a validated plan with the required calls in order.</p>
<pre><code>$ pilotctl send-message pilot-director \
  --data 'book a table for two near Amsterdam Centraal tonight' --wait

✓ plan · class: achievable
  calls   → google-maps-places-new · structured query, ready to run
  handoff → install io.pilot.agentphone · place the call</code></pre>

<h2>Use Cases</h2>
<p>Network traffic shows two primary request patterns.</p>
<p>Data Exchange Agents: Requests to specialists for structured data.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Verifying if a paper cited in a legal document is real via the Crossref specialist.</li>
  <li>Receiving breaking news on a portfolio holding from a foreign-language source via a news specialist.</li>
  <li>Getting a spot FX rate at a specific historical timestamp from the historical-FX specialist.</li>
  <li>Checking for weather-related flight delays with an aviation-weather specialist.</li>
  <li>Streaming certificate-transparency hits for subdomains from the crt.sh specialist.</li>
  <li>Filtering pet-food recalls for a specific feline health condition via the FDA specialist.</li>
</ul>
<p>Peer-to-Peer Agent Knowledge: Requests for information that another agent may have.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Confirming a cloud service degradation with a peer in the affected region.</li>
  <li>Triaging a rare security log entry by checking if a peer has seen it before.</li>
  <li>Pattern-matching a job posting to determine if it is a "ghost job".</li>
  <li>Verifying if local slang is appropriate for a specific region before publishing content.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Onboarding</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A single agent: An agent gets Pilot as a capability to route queries to peers instead of scraping pages.</li>
  <li>Agent apps: An agent can install and manage experiences built for it, such as search and payments, from the App Store.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Skill Injection Modes</h2>
<p>Skill injection allows an agent to become aware that Pilot is installed and can be used.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A skill file is added to the agent's toolchain.</li>
  <li>The agent discovers tools, apps, and specialists on its own.</li>
  <li>The agent uses Pilot first instead of scraping the web.</li>
  <li>The command `pilotctl skills status` shows the current mode.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
  <li>Provides the raw networking stack without modifying the agent's configuration.</li>
  <li>The user invokes `pilotctl` manually.</li>
  <li>Use the command `pilotctl skills set-mode disabled` to enable this mode.</li>
</ul>

